# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DISABLED ON PURPOSE. `.disabled` because ArgoCD's directory recursion only
# reads .yaml/.yml/.json, so this file is inert until it is renamed — the same
# mechanism tabula.yaml.disabled uses.
#
# It is NOT disabled because it is unfinished. It is disabled because five
# required values do not exist yet, and every one of them is a security
# boundary rather than a setting. The chart refuses to render without them
# (see mcp-slack/deploy/chart/templates/_helpers.tpl), so enabling this early
# would produce a failed sync rather than an exposed endpoint — but a failed
# sync on a publicly-routed hostname is still not a state worth entering.
#
# A sixth, networkPolicy.enabled, is required in the same fail-closed sense but
# is a DECISION rather than a lookup — see criterion 7. It has no placeholder
# to fill from elsewhere; someone has to choose, and the render aborts until
# they do.
#
# TO ENABLE, all EIGHT must be true:
#
#   1. image.digest — the mcp-slack image is built and pushed, and you have
#      its sha256 digest. Never a tag: an internet-facing workload must run
#      the artifact that was reviewed, not whatever the tag moved to.
#
#      MET as of this commit, at `sha256:303c712c…`, whose
#      org.opencontainers.image.revision is `2998affe` (#1514).
#
#      DO NOT WRITE A NEW PR NUMBER INTO THIS TEST. Earlier versions asserted
#      "contains #1499's merge commit". That is true of every image built
#      after #1499 landed, forever, so it passes against an arbitrarily stale
#      digest and cannot express the property anyone actually cares about.
#      This pin has been wrong once already for exactly that reason. The test
#      below has no identifier in it and does not decay.
#
#      TWO INDEPENDENT ASSERTIONS. The first binds the digest to a commit; the
#      second says that commit is current. Both are needed — a correct
#      artifact can be superseded, and a current commit says nothing about
#      what was published from it.
#
#      A. ARTIFACT BINDING — read from the registry, anonymously, no build:
#         - org.opencontainers.image.revision on BOTH platform manifests,
#           read separately. They must agree and must name a commit on `main`.
#         - `git merge-base --is-ancestor <that revision> origin/main` exits 0.
#           Exit 0 alone is also what a test that can never fail looks like, so
#           show the test can say no — but run that control against a KNOWN-OLDER
#           revision (`is-ancestor origin/main 03cf6dab` → 1), NOT by reversing
#           the arguments on the pinned revision. When the pin is `main`'s tip —
#           the normal state right after a re-pin — BOTH directions exit 0,
#           because a commit is its own ancestor. Same degeneracy as B below,
#           from the same trigger (`R == origin/main`), and it has to be checked
#           separately for each arm: catching it in one says nothing about the
#           other. Aegis's catch on the first draft of this very paragraph,
#           which asserted the reverse "must exit 1" and did not reproduce.
#         - the index carries spdx.dev/Document alongside
#           slsa.dev/provenance/v1, on both platforms. Positive control: the
#           pre-#1504 image de6e4203 carries provenance and NOTHING else, so
#           this arm distinguishes rather than always agreeing.
#
#      B. CURRENCY — nothing newer has been published:
#           paths := the `on.push.paths` list, read from
#                    `git show origin/main:.github/workflows/mcp-slack-image.yaml`
#                    — from the REF, never the working tree, which in a
#                    worktree or a parked checkout is a third copy that can
#                    disagree with both sides. Assert the list is non-empty.
#           git rev-list --count <revision>..origin/main -- <paths>   == 0
#         Build that argv directly (python `subprocess`), never via a shell.
#         Every shell form of this has been measured wrong in some
#         environment: a `$VAR` round-trip doesn't word-split in zsh; an
#         inline `$(...)` gets glob-expanded by bash against the working tree,
#         which silently drops `mcp-slack/.github/**` because `*` skips
#         dotfiles. All of them fail toward 0, i.e. "nothing newer".
#
#      B IS VACUOUS IMMEDIATELY AFTER A RE-PIN AND THAT IS NOT A NIT. When the
#      pinned revision IS `origin/main`, the range is empty before any filter
#      applies, so B returns 0 for a working derivation, a dead one, a garbage
#      pathspec, and no pathspec at all. It is asserting that a commit is not
#      ahead of itself. B discriminates only while the pin is already stale —
#      it goes blind at the exact moment someone re-pins and most wants to
#      certify the result. **So run B against a known-stale revision in the
#      same invocation as a live control** (`03cf6dab` returns 3 here, and 0
#      under a bogus pathspec). Without that row, a broken derivation passes
#      unnoticed and then keeps returning 0 after `main` moves, when 0 has
#      become wrong.
#
#      WHY NOT "the publish that fired after the PR merged": the merge queue
#      batches up to 5 entries (ruleset 17645603, `max_entries_to_merge: 5`,
#      `min_entries_to_merge_wait_minutes: 5`), and only the batch TIP fires a
#      `push`. Measured twice: #1499 published under #1505's commit, #1509
#      under #1513's. Looking for "PR N's image" finds nothing and the absence
#      is the batch, not a failure. Assertion A's revision label is the only
#      reliable link from artifact back to commit.
#
#      PIN THE INDEX DIGEST, NOT A PLATFORM MANIFEST. `_helpers.tpl` emits
#      `repository@digest` verbatim, and this cluster is mixed-arch; pinning
#      `sha256:a718e0be…` (amd64) would fail to schedule on arm64 nodes AND
#      orphan the SBOM, because the attestations hang off the index.
#
#      NOT COVERED BY THIS CRITERION: there are no `imagePullSecrets` anywhere
#      in mcp-slack/deploy, so the pull is anonymous and works only because the
#      GHCR package is public.
#
#      Everything below cites STEP NAMES and comment headers, never absolute
#      line numbers. That is deliberate: a comment-only PR elsewhere in the
#      tree shifts line numbers without changing behaviour, so nothing in
#      review and no check flags it, and a durable comment quietly starts
#      pointing at the wrong place. Not hypothetical: this paragraph was
#      drafted citing the gate step by number, and #1509 — a comment-only PR,
#      in flight at the same time — inserted 28 lines above it and moved it.
#      Caught by Ridge before either landed. A step name survives exactly the
#      edit that breaks a number.
#
#      CORRECTION to what #1510 landed here, which said "nothing asserts it".
#      That was wrong, and wrong in the direction that costs a reader work: it
#      invites building a control that already exists, or adding an
#      imagePullSecret that this workload deliberately does not have.
#
#        - The absence is a DESIGN CHOICE, not an oversight.
#          `.github/workflows/mcp-slack-image.yaml`, under the header "WHY GHCR
#          AND NOT ARTIFACT REGISTRY", argues it: this image
#          is consumed by the k3s homelab, which has no ambient cloud identity,
#          so Artifact Registry would make a pull secret MANDATORY — a
#          credential to seal and rotate forever. A public GHCR package needs
#          none. Adding `imagePullSecrets` would put a registry credential on
#          an internet-facing pod to protect against a state nobody has chosen.
#        - The property IS asserted, by an anonymous request — the only
#          instrument that can answer it, since an authenticated check succeeds
#          on a private package. `mcp-slack-image.yaml`'s step "Verify the
#          image is anonymously pullable" exits 1 with the `gh api … -f
#          visibility=public` remedy, and the "ASSERTION 4" comment directly
#          above that step names THIS chart's missing `imagePullSecrets` as
#          the reason it matters.
#
#      The real gap is narrower and is about WHEN, not whether — see #1512.
#      Assertion 4 reads `steps.publish.outputs.digest`, so it is bound to a
#      publish: it asserts the package was public AT PUBLISH TIME, for the
#      digest that publish created. ArgoCD pulls on every sync, restart,
#      reschedule and node replacement, forever, off a digest pinned in git,
#      and a visibility flip generates no publish event — so for an
#      already-pinned digest that gate never runs again. Because this chart
#      pins by digest, a healthy deployment can go a long time with no
#      `mcp-slack/**` push at all.
#
#      `workflow_dispatch` IS NOT the re-check it looks like. Dispatch takes
#      the same `!= 'pull_request'` branch as "Build and push" at
#      mcp-slack-image.yaml's step "Build and push (multi-platform)", so it
#      builds and pushes a NEW image and
#      verifies that digest, moving `:main` in the process. Re-verifying the
#      currently pinned digest is not expressible today without publishing a
#      new one. Aegis's catch, and the reason "just dispatch the workflow" is
#      the wrong answer under incident.
#
#      SO THE FIRST SIGNAL IS GENERIC AND ~15 MINUTES LATE. All three
#      mcp-slack alerts are Envoy-traffic-derived, and a pod that never starts
#      serves no requests, so none of them fires (they are at
#      McpSlackSustained5xx, McpSlackIdpUnavailable and
#      McpSlackSustainedForbidden, in platform/prometheus/applicationset.yaml).
#      `PodNotReadyLong` in that same file (`for: 15m`, phase Pending|Unknown)
#      does — an ImagePullBackOff pod is `Pending`.
#      `NodeContainersStuckWaiting` needs >= 3 waiting
#      containers on a node, so `replicas: 1` will not trip it. A
#      registry-permissions change therefore presents as "has not reached
#      Running for >15m", not as anything naming the registry.
#
#      IF YOU ARE HERE UNDER INCIDENT: the chart has no ServiceAccount template
#      and no `serviceAccountName`, so the pod runs on the namespace default
#      SA. That is a place a pull secret can be attached out-of-band, which
#      means invisibly to GitOps — do not fix it there. Note that
#      `automountServiceAccountToken: false` in the chart's deployment.yaml
#      pod spec does NOT
#      close that door: pull secrets are applied from the ServiceAccount by
#      admission regardless of whether its token is mounted. #1512's remedy is an
#      empty-default `imagePullSecrets` hook in the chart so the fix is a
#      values change, plus a scheduled re-assertion that reads `image.digest`
#      out of THIS file and runs the token+manifest check against that digest
#      with no build and no push.
#
#      Adding a pull secret pre-emptively is explicitly NOT the remedy: the
#      package is public, measured by three of us on two instruments, and a
#      registry credential nobody needs on an internet-facing pod is its own
#      liability.
#
#      Tracked on #1512 rather than #1507: that one is "the gate cannot fail
#      because the package is public", this is "the pull only works because
#      the package is public" — same property, opposite dependents, different
#      remedies. Beacon's call on the split, and correct.
#
#   2. oidc.projectId — `pulumi stack output projectId` on the
#      zitadel-apps-mcp-slack stack (PR #1417), which has not been applied.
#      Hand-typing this defeats the per-project audience isolation the
#      dedicated Zitadel project exists to provide.
#
#   3. slack.channelIds — real channel IDs, which requires the Slack admin
#      session that has not happened. Note the standing constraint: no real
#      channel goes in here while the endpoint is publicly reachable and
#      caller identity is unenforced. That is a sequencing rule, not a
#      preference — the allow-list bounds which conversations a caller can
#      reach, and it is doing that work alone until caller identity lands.
#
#      That rule got stricter, not looser, now that the app manifest carries
#      groups:read and groups:history: the list gates PRIVATE channels too,
#      so the thing it is holding back alone is private-conversation
#      disclosure. Public-only would have been a defensible risk to carry for
#      a short PoC window. This is not.
#
#   4. The Secret `mcp-slack-tokens` exists in the mcp-slack namespace,
#      carrying SLACK_BOT_TOKEN and SLACK_TEAM_ID and nothing else. Created
#      out-of-band via a SealedSecret (the ntfy alertmanager-ntfy-endpoint
#      precedent) because it needs that same Slack admin session. No user
#      token is ever mounted here.
#
#   5. THE BOT IS ALREADY IN EVERY CHANNEL NAMED IN slack.channelIds, AND
#      THE INVITE HAPPENS BEFORE THE DEPLOY, NOT AFTER.
#
#      The intuitive order — add the ID, deploy, then invite the bot — does
#      not work and does not look like a missing step when it fails. Since
#      #1421 the server verifies each allow-listed conversation's visibility
#      at startup, and `conversations.info` on a private channel the bot has
#      not joined returns `channel_not_found`, so the process exits non-zero.
#      With replicas: 1 and self-heal that presents as CrashLoopBackOff —
#      i.e. "the deploy is broken", pointing at the chart rather than at the
#      skipped invite. Aegis's catch on #1421.
#
#   6. A REVERSIBLE fast containment lever exists — currently the Cloudflare
#      WAF/rate-limit rule (criterion 3, James's account). Not "a stop button"
#      but a reversible one: revoking the Slack bot token also stops the
#      endpoint, but restoring it needs a re-seal and a deploy, so under a CI
#      outage it is a one-way door. The test to apply to any lever proposed
#      later is whether UNDOING it requires CI.
#
#      THIS RULE IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN IaC, and "no managed path exists" is
#      too weak a reason — it reads as a limitation, which invites someone to
#      fix it. The actual argument, which a future proposal has to defeat:
#
#      Codifying it makes the ENABLED state a managed field. Toggle the rule
#      on in the console during an incident and that is drift, so the next
#      apply turns your containment back off — the reconciler-undoes-your-
#      deviation property this lever exists to escape. Avoid that with
#      IgnoreChanges on the enabled field and you have rebuilt
#      `accessTokenType` from zitadel-apps-mcp-slack: a security-relevant
#      setting the stack will never reconcile, reporting clean forever while
#      the live value is whatever someone last set by hand. That stack needed
#      a dedicated output just to detect it.
#
#      So unmanaged is not the compromise here; it is the only shape with no
#      reconciler between an operator and the stop button. Aegis's framing.
#
#      Narrowly scoped on purpose: this applies to the enabled state of an
#      EMERGENCY control, not to configuration generally. Nobody contains a
#      breach by editing a token lifetime, so it does not license unmanaged
#      config elsewhere.
#
#   7. THE EGRESS CiliumNetworkPolicy ACTUALLY PASSES TRAFFIC. IT DOES NOT
#      TODAY — measured 2026-08-06 (`7711924c`), not suspected.
#
#      Cilium's L7 DNS proxy does not answer on this cluster, so with the
#      policy applied the pod cannot resolve anything — including the two
#      names the rule explicitly allows. Widening to `matchPattern: "*"`
#      does not help. Dropping `rules.dns` restores DNS but then `toFQDNs`
#      matches nothing, because the proxy is what populates the FQDN→IP
#      mapping, so `:443` is blocked instead. Both ways out are closed.
#      Tracked as a platform defect in #1428.
#
#      IF YOU ARE HERE BECAUSE SLACK CALLS FAIL AND THE CNP LOOKS LIKE THE
#      CAUSE: it is the cause, and deleting it does fix it. DO NOT DELETE IT.
#      That policy is the only control that survives compromise of the bot
#      token — every other one on this endpoint is enforced by our own
#      process and is void the moment the token leaves the pod. Removing it
#      leaves an internet-facing pod holding a Slack credential with no
#      egress bounding at all, and it will look like a successful fix.
#      Escalate on #1428 instead.
#
#      The chart makes this a value rather than a warning: `networkPolicy.
#      enabled` must be set explicitly to a boolean or the render aborts.
#      #1487 (merged 2026-08-08) fixed this: the CNP now pins DNS to kube-dns
#      at L3/L4 with no `rules.dns` block, so it passes traffic without
#      depending on the broken L7 DNS proxy. `true` is the correct value —
#      it costs nothing against #1428 (no bound exists for :443/DNS-content
#      exfil either way) and buys the entire lateral bound (apiserver, other
#      namespaces, Postgres, the Buzz relay — NOT Zitadel: the `world` :443
#      rule stays open deliberately so the pod can reach the IdP's JWKS on
#      every token verification). `false` is now the dangerous choice: zero
#      in-cluster containment on an internet-facing
#      pod holding a Slack token. This criterion is met once it is set true.
#
#      Written as a numbered criterion rather than left in the template's
#      header because criteria 1-8 are the list someone reads before
#      enabling, and this is the item most likely to be acted on wrongly by
#      someone who never opens the template. Aegis's ask on #1420.
#
#   8. oidc.allowedSubjects — the comma-separated Zitadel subjects (NUMERIC
#      user ids) permitted to call this endpoint.
#
#      Added late, and the omission is the point: this criterion list ran to
#      seven items across eight commits while the chart already refused to
#      render without this value. A checklist that is shorter than the set of
#      things that block the render is worse than no checklist — it reads as
#      complete, so the reader stops when they reach the end of it.
#
#      It is NOT a duplicate of criterion 2. projectId is the audience check,
#      and the audience check is not an identity check: `aud` is caller-
#      authored — any authenticated principal can add this project to it with
#      a scope string — so issuer + audience together admit every account this
#      Zitadel instance will issue a token to. THIS is the only per-request
#      identity control. Criterion 2 being satisfied says nothing about this
#      one.
#
#      Nothing outside this repo has to happen first, unlike 1-5: the ids come
#      from Zitadel and can be read today. That makes it the cheapest of the
#      eight, which is its own hazard — cheap items get assumed done.
#
# NOT YET A NUMBERED CRITERION: oidc.clientId (#1491) is also render-blocking
# as of this file's "helm" values block below — work criteria 1-8 top to
# bottom, satisfy all eight, still get a failed render. Full write-up is
# item 4's job; this line exists so the header — "the list someone reads
# before enabling" (criterion 7's own words) — doesn't silently omit it.
#
# The alert for this workload is NOT in the chart — it lives in
# gitops/argocd/platform/prometheus/applicationset.yaml, group `mcp-slack`.
# This cluster has no prometheus-operator, so a PrometheusRule would sync
# Healthy and never load. See the chart's values.yaml for the full note.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: mcp-slack
  namespace: argocd
  annotations:
    # Wave 2, same as cloudflared/whoami/ntfy: the Gateway (envoy-gateway) and
    # the tunnel (cloudflared) must be up before this HTTPRoute can resolve.
    argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: "2"
spec:
  # Scoped per-app project, not the shared applications-project — see
  # gitops/argocd/projects/mcp-slack-project.yaml.
  project: mcp-slack-project
  source:
    # Git-source Helm, NOT the OCI chart pattern tabula uses. Deliberate: an
    # OCI chart has to be published by CI before it can deploy, which adds a
    # release step between "merged" and "reconciling" and lets the deployed
    # chart lag the reviewed one. Rendering the chart straight from the
    # monorepo keeps git the single source of truth, which is the property
    # this platform is supposed to have.
    repoURL: https://github.com/VitruvianSoftware/vitruvian-core.git
    targetRevision: main
    path: mcp-slack/deploy/chart
    helm:
      values: |
        # SIX VALUES ARE RENDER-BLOCKING. FOUR are still empty placeholders below.
        # `networkPolicy.enabled` is filled (#1497 — #1487 fixed the CNP, so `true`
        # is now the correct value, see criterion 7) and `image.digest` is filled
        # (criterion 1, which carries the four assertions that make this
        # particular digest the correct one — read it before replacing the
        # value). `oidc.clientId` (#1491) pins the caller to this server's own
        # OIDC client — added to the count, not yet to the go-live criteria list
        # above, which is item 4's job. The chart fails to render on any of the
        # six being empty/unset — that is the intended behaviour, not an obstacle
        # to work around. Fill the rest from the sources named above.
        #
        # ALL SIX ARE NOW RENDER-VERIFIED, not merely read: #1502's `chart-render`
        # CI job nulls each required value in turn and asserts the render aborts
        # naming that value — `oidc.clientId` included. That job runs on every
        # PR, so this block cannot drift from `_helpers.tpl` silently again.
        # Helm reports only the FIRST missing value, so a block that is short by
        # two looks exactly like a block that is short by one — which is how
        # this one carried three entries for eight commits while the chart
        # required five. If you add a `required` to the chart, add its
        # placeholder here in the same commit — `chart-render`'s negative
        # controls are extracted from the templates, so a new one is
        # automatically gated even if this comment is forgotten.
        image:
          # FILLED — criterion 1. INDEX digest of ghcr.io/vitruviansoftware/
          # mcp-slack, revision label 2998affe on both platform manifests.
          # Not a platform manifest digest, not a tag. Replacing this means
          # re-running criterion 1's assertions A and B against the new
          # digest — including B's known-stale control row, without which B
          # cannot tell a working check from a dead one.
          digest: "sha256:303c712cc201da8dcda4ad3f42f063bc9cc0abf22f4e379b8bb72cc5550afd34"
        oidc:
          projectId: ""         # pulumi stack output projectId — REQUIRED
          allowedSubjects: ""   # numeric Zitadel user ids — REQUIRED
          clientId: ""          # pulumi stack output clientId — REQUIRED (#1491)
        slack:
          channelIds: ""        # comma-separated channel IDs — REQUIRED
        networkPolicy:
          # REQUIRED, and a real boolean — a quoted "false" is refused, because
          # Helm treats any non-empty string as true. See criterion 7: #1487
          # fixed the CNP, so true is now the correct value — it costs nothing
          # against #1428 and buys the entire lateral bound. false means NOTHING
          # constrains where the bot token can be sent and is the choice to avoid.
          enabled: true
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: mcp-slack
  # CONTAINMENT NOTE — READ BEFORE REACHING FOR kubectl IN AN INCIDENT.
  #
  # selfHeal is on, and this cluster reconciles every 120s with 60s of jitter
  # (argocd-cm: timeout.reconciliation 120s, timeout.reconciliation.jitter
  # 60s). So `kubectl scale deploy/mcp-slack --replicas=0` and
  # `kubectl delete httproute mcp-slack` DO NOT CONTAIN ANYTHING — both are
  # desired state here, and ArgoCD reverts them within ~2-3 minutes.
  #
  # That is the worst shape a containment action can have: it appears to work,
  # then silently undoes itself on a timer while someone believes the endpoint
  # is down.
  #
  # THERE ARE TWO RECONCILERS, AND SUSPENDING ONLY THIS APPLICATION IS NOT
  # ENOUGH. `app-of-applications` (gitops/argocd/root-applications.yaml) also
  # runs selfHeal: true and owns this Application object, so patching this
  # file's syncPolicy is itself reverted by the root app-of-apps. Verified
  # against the live root Application, which lists every leaf as a managed
  # resource.
  #
  # To actually stop traffic fast, suspend from the ROOT DOWN, then act:
  #
  #   kubectl patch application app-of-applications -n argocd --type=json \
  #     -p '[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/syncPolicy/automated"}]'
  #   kubectl patch application mcp-slack -n argocd --type=json \
  #     -p '[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/syncPolicy/automated"}]'
  #   kubectl scale deploy/mcp-slack -n mcp-slack --replicas=0
  #
  # Seconds, and it survives, because nothing is reconciling any layer back.
  # Restore in reverse order. Skipping the first command produces the exact
  # failure this note exists to prevent, one level up: the suspend appears to
  # work and is quietly undone.
  #
  # The alternative — revert the git commit — is correct and durable but runs
  # at GitOps speed (commit, CI, sync, rollout), which is minutes and depends
  # on CI being up. Prefer it for anything non-urgent; it leaves git as the
  # source of truth rather than a cluster that silently disagrees with it.
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true
    syncOptions:
      - CreateNamespace=true
      - ServerSideApply=true
